WEBVTT

00:01.040 --> 00:05.390
I'd like to leave you with some very important takeaways in terms of scope.

00:05.600 --> 00:07.790
Here are the things that I want you to remember.

00:08.000 --> 00:10.340
Defining scope forces everybody.

00:10.340 --> 00:10.570
Okay?

00:10.580 --> 00:16.730
All players on your team, the clients team to see and address potential conflicts and rough spots before

00:16.730 --> 00:18.770
time is invested in designing or building.

00:18.770 --> 00:22.160
That's the reason the scope phase exists at all.

00:22.160 --> 00:29.270
It allows you to identify all those things that have the potential to derail your progress, to derail

00:29.270 --> 00:31.970
the value of the project or the product.

00:31.970 --> 00:35.030
And you recognize it up front so that you can make some adjustments.

00:35.030 --> 00:36.980
You can say, okay, well then we're not going to do that.

00:36.980 --> 00:38.390
We're going to do it this other way.

00:38.510 --> 00:42.590
It's really, really important to take the time to figure out what your scope is.

00:43.120 --> 00:48.880
The best possible source for requirements are always going to be the people who use the system, the

00:48.880 --> 00:49.510
users.

00:49.510 --> 00:55.030
But you have to remember that what they say they need is not always what they actually need.

00:55.030 --> 00:59.830
And in addition to that, there may be things that they don't know they need.

01:00.250 --> 01:07.540
All content should be user centered, and it all has major impact on user experience.

01:07.540 --> 01:14.380
The decisions that you make with regard to content absolutely have a trickle down effect to everything

01:14.380 --> 01:17.170
that happens after you define what the content is.

01:18.030 --> 01:20.820
Remember that requirements are not gathered.

01:20.850 --> 01:22.170
They're generated.

01:22.170 --> 01:24.510
There is a huge, huge, huge difference.

01:24.510 --> 01:27.300
And most of the time they're negotiated.

01:27.330 --> 01:29.820
That also is incredibly important.

01:29.820 --> 01:37.560
Realize that this is, to some degree a balancing act between you, the client, internal forces, external

01:37.560 --> 01:38.520
forces.

01:38.520 --> 01:39.960
It's a negotiation.

01:39.960 --> 01:40.260
Okay.

01:40.260 --> 01:47.040
So it's okay if in the first one hour session you don't know everything there is to know about what

01:47.040 --> 01:47.970
your requirements are?

01:48.000 --> 01:50.340
It's a process and you have to go through it.

01:51.160 --> 01:53.770
Use scenarios are your best friend.

01:53.800 --> 02:00.580
They enable you to generate requirements faster than any other process I have ever used.

02:00.580 --> 02:08.140
And I say that because I've been doing this for 22 years and by and large, the majority of what I spend

02:08.140 --> 02:13.960
my time doing is creating use scenarios because we get more valuable information from two hours of doing

02:13.960 --> 02:21.880
that than we typically get, you know, in eight hours of typical, you know, requirements, meetings.

02:22.450 --> 02:28.720
The most critical requirements and specifications that you will have should align with strategic objectives.

02:28.720 --> 02:35.410
And if they don't, there is a very good chance they're not important because nobody really understands

02:35.410 --> 02:37.000
why they're there in the first place.

02:37.630 --> 02:41.690
Remember that every force evolves requirements.

02:41.710 --> 02:45.400
You don't just come up with a list and set it and forget it once you come up with the list.

02:45.430 --> 02:49.900
Then you've got to think about, okay, well, what are all the other things that could possibly affect

02:49.900 --> 02:52.300
whether we can or can't do these things?
